---
term: Externality
section: coordination
order: 10
definition: An effect of a corporation's actions on interests outside its legitimate jurisdiction.
example: An upstream mining corporation's runoff harms a downstream farming corporation. The shared river-basin corporation takes up the case and sets discharge limits that both must follow.
seeAlso:
  - arbitration
  - parent-corporation
---
When a corporation's actions reach beyond its charter, the affected parties can [[escalation|escalate]] the matter to a parent or higher-order corporation whose jurisdiction covers both sides. Higher-order corporations exist partly because constituents can't always optimize independently without imposing costs on one another.

People who bear small, scattered costs are often the least likely to organize and escalate. Each parent's charter states whether it waits for escalation, takes up externalities on its own initiative, or finds them through an [[audit]].
